Description: DFIR bot FAST triage — what the bot can actually reason about, without the slow bulk
Author: DFIR-project
Version: 1.2
Id: 7a1c0001-0a0a-4b0a-9001-000000000001
RecreateDirectories: true
# 2026-08-22. Measured before changing anything:
#   * KapeTriage ALREADY contains WebBrowsers, RemoteAdmin, PowerShellConsole, SRUM, SUM,
#     EventLogs, FileSystem, RegistryHives, EvidenceOfExecution, LNK/JumpLists, WBEM,
#     ScheduledTasks, RecycleBin, WER, WindowsTimeline and CloudStorage_Metadata - so three
#     of the seven entries in v1.1 were repeating what was already collected;
#   * RemoteAdmin is itself a compound of THIRTY remote-access tools (AnyDesk, RustDesk,
#     TeamViewer, RMS, Radmin, UltraViewer, QuickAssist, Remcos, ScreenConnect...), which
#     the bot's threat detection hunts for by name - FAST was never missing those;
#   * what FAST genuinely lacked were small, high-value artefacts that only FULL carried.
# So: drop the repetition, add what is cheap to collect and answers a question the report
# asks. Nothing large was added - ThumbCache and RoamingProfile stay FULL-only on purpose.
Targets:
    -
        Name: KapeTriage
        Category: Targets
        Path: KapeTriage.tkape
    -
        Name: WindowsDefender
        Category: Targets
        Path: WindowsDefender.tkape
    -
        Name: BITS
        Category: Targets
        Path: BITS.tkape
    -
        Name: RDPCache
        Category: Targets
        Path: RDPCache.tkape
    # --- added 2026-08-22: small, and each one answers something the report asks -------
    -
        # what was actually typed into PowerShell; the console history in KapeTriage only
        # shows the last lines per user, transcripts show whole sessions
        Name: PowerShellTranscripts
        Category: Targets
        Path: PowerShellTranscripts.tkape
    -
        # which RDP sessions were opened FROM this machine - the other half of RDPCache
        Name: RDPJumplist
        Category: Targets
        Path: RDPJumplist.tkape
    -
        # USB history: the exfiltration route that leaves no network trace
        Name: USBDetective
        Category: Targets
        Path: USBDetective.tkape
    -
        # VPN client configs and logs - how the operator got in, or got out
        Name: VPNClients
        Category: Targets
        Path: VPNClients.tkape
    -
        # the notification database keeps message previews long after the app is gone
        Name: WindowsNotificationsDB
        Category: Targets
        Path: WindowsNotificationsDB.tkape
    -
        # network profiles and interface history
        Name: WindowsNetwork
        Category: Targets
        Path: WindowsNetwork.tkape
    -
        # FTP client: the plainest exfiltration tool there is
        Name: FileZillaClient
        Category: Targets
        Path: FileZillaClient.tkape
